WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.210
DWAYNE "THE ROCK" JOHNSON: Boat a boat!

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:06.131
The gods have given me a--

00:00:07.298 --> 00:00:10.510
NARRATOR: In recent years,
big actors like Chris Pratt

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.471
The Rock, and Margot
Robbie, are taking on roles

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:15.765
in animated movies more and more.

00:00:15.765 --> 00:00:17.058
ANNOUNCER: Actors and actresses

00:00:17.058 --> 00:00:19.853
perform for unseen audiences.

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:22.397
NARRATOR: You can see the
appeal, roll up to work

00:00:22.397 --> 00:00:25.233
in your pajamas, and say a
few lines into a microphone

00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:26.776
seems like a sweet gig, right?

00:00:26.776 --> 00:00:29.571
JOAN: It actually takes
months of training,

00:00:29.571 --> 00:00:30.739
and sometimes years

00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:33.616
and when I've worked with
people that are on Broadway

00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:36.578
and celebrities, almost
always, and these are actors

00:00:36.578 --> 00:00:40.749
and almost always, they'll
say to me, I had no idea.

00:00:40.749 --> 00:00:42.250
NARRATOR: So, how exactly do

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.544
actors prepare for animated roles?

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:47.172
We went straight to the
experts to find out.

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:53.720
RUDY: Hi, I'm Rudy Gaskins,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.473
CEO of the Society of
Voice Arts and Sciences

00:00:56.473 --> 00:00:58.433
here with my partner, Joan, to talk

00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:00.810
about all things voice acting.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.020
NARRATOR: Joan and Rudy have been

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.356
teaching their craft for over 25 years.

00:01:05.356 --> 00:01:09.819
RUDY: We've worked with
Phil LaMarr, who's one of

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:12.238
the voices of Family
Guy and Nancy Cartwright

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:13.948
who's the voice of Bart Simpson.

00:01:13.948 --> 00:01:15.283
NANCY CARTWRIGHT: Your
attention please, your

00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:17.327
attention please. I have
an announcement to make.

00:01:17.327 --> 00:01:18.787
RUDY: You wouldn't believe
a 60 year old woman

00:01:18.787 --> 00:01:21.081
is that adolescent's voice,

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:23.083
but she is and she's extraordinary.

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:25.752
NARRATOR: But talent is
only part of the equation.

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:28.046
When it's just your
voice doing all the work

00:01:28.046 --> 00:01:31.216
you have to make sure that
instrument is in tip top shape.

00:01:31.216 --> 00:01:34.844
Voice coaches lead actors
through a variety of exercises

00:01:34.844 --> 00:01:36.721
to optimize their vocal chords

00:01:36.721 --> 00:01:39.057
and condition their mouth muscles.

00:01:39.057 --> 00:01:41.267
Some of these are things you might expect

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:42.602
like controlling your breathing

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.979
and learning to speak on the breath.

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:46.773
JOAN: Hi, how are you?

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:49.025
NARRATOR: Some of them are less expected

00:01:49.025 --> 00:01:50.985
like the jaw, throat, and tongue warmups

00:01:50.985 --> 00:01:53.488
that actors do before
they get in the booth.

00:01:53.488 --> 00:01:56.199
JOAN: Most people, their
tension is in their jaw.

00:01:56.199 --> 00:02:00.662
So, I'm using these
fingers to hold the jaw

00:02:00.662 --> 00:02:02.580
not clench the jaw, but hold it

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.041
and I'm gonna take a
diaphragmatic breath in.

00:02:05.041 --> 00:02:06.292
So if I go...

00:02:13.007 --> 00:02:14.467
Now I'm gonna do it on sound.

00:02:20.098 --> 00:02:24.727
It's key to have a relaxed
open back of the throat,

00:02:24.727 --> 00:02:29.440
as opposed to a tense and
fixed back of the throat,

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.027
which means that sometimes
the breath can't go out

00:02:33.027 --> 00:02:36.406
the mouth so it has to
go shoot out the nose.

00:02:36.406 --> 00:02:39.159
And that's when you get
things like nasality.

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:40.451
NARRATOR: Nasal might actually

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:42.203
work for certain characters.

00:02:42.203 --> 00:02:43.872
OWEN WILSON: Of course
I wanna keep racing.

00:02:43.872 --> 00:02:45.373
BOB BERGEN: Th-this is my backyard.

00:02:45.373 --> 00:02:47.417
NARRATOR: But in most
roles, actors wanna speak

00:02:47.417 --> 00:02:49.878
more deeply and roundly so their voices

00:02:49.878 --> 00:02:53.256
can capture a fuller range
of emotional expression.

00:02:53.256 --> 00:02:56.843
WILL ARNETT: Outta
curiosity, why wouldn't you

00:02:56.843 --> 00:03:01.306
wanna marry me? Just, you know,
again, purely for curiosity.

00:03:01.306 --> 00:03:04.184
JOAN: The little dingy thing
in the back is called the

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:08.855
uvula. Most people's are frozen
and they are kinda stuck.

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:11.065
So there's exercises like...

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:16.738
You'll hear voiceover
people do that in abundance.

00:03:16.738 --> 00:03:19.908
And it's to exercise
the uvula in the back,

00:03:19.908 --> 00:03:21.242
so that it's almost like

00:03:21.242 --> 00:03:23.328
a punching bag the way it moves.

00:03:23.328 --> 00:03:24.913
NARRATOR: Clever tongue twisters are

00:03:24.913 --> 00:03:28.082
another key part of
actor's warmup routines.

00:03:28.082 --> 00:03:29.751
JOAN: What everyone knows is Peter Piper

00:03:29.751 --> 00:03:31.878
picked a peck of pickled peppers.

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:33.922
But there is a lot of tongue twisters

00:03:33.922 --> 00:03:36.507
that aren't necessarily long,

00:03:36.507 --> 00:03:40.386
but they really help nip in
the bud certain pronunciations.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:43.306
Abominable abdominals,
abominable abdominals,

00:03:43.306 --> 00:03:46.017
abominable abdominals,
abominable abdominals.

00:03:46.017 --> 00:03:48.269
Kinky cookie, kinky cookie, kinky cookie.

00:03:48.269 --> 00:03:51.481
Lemon lime liniment, lemon lime liniment.

00:03:51.481 --> 00:03:54.234
Eleven benevolent elephants, try it.

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.778
CAMERAMAN: Eleven benevel...

00:03:56.778 --> 00:03:58.821
I don't even think I can
say that word normally.

00:03:59.864 --> 00:04:01.491
RUDY: Yeah, that's why it's in there.

00:04:01.491 --> 00:04:04.369
NARRATOR: These exercises
help to relieve tension.

00:04:04.369 --> 00:04:07.538
But even some seasoned
actors will still get nervous

00:04:07.538 --> 00:04:09.415
once they hop in front of the mic.

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:11.918
JOAN: Their throat gets
dry, their tongue gets dry.

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:14.337
When you talking there's a lot of

00:04:14.337 --> 00:04:16.381
lip smacking.

00:04:16.381 --> 00:04:19.050
What I'll do is I will eat a green apple.

00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:21.678
NARRATOR: The acids that give green apples

00:04:21.678 --> 00:04:25.473
their sour taste also can
stimulate saliva production.

00:04:25.473 --> 00:04:27.475
This helps clean and moisten the mouth,

00:04:27.475 --> 00:04:30.395
reducing problems like lip
smacking and mic clicks.

00:04:30.395 --> 00:04:32.647
Some people have the opposite problem.

00:04:32.647 --> 00:04:35.858
They produce too much saliva
when they're worked up.

00:04:35.858 --> 00:04:37.110
JOAN: So they sound like they're

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.445
a little drunk, but they're nervous.

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.322
NARRATOR: Luckily there's
a quick fix for actors

00:04:41.322 --> 00:04:43.074
who hyper-salivate in front of the mic.

00:04:43.074 --> 00:04:45.535
JOAN: What I tell them to
do is take coffee grinds,

00:04:45.535 --> 00:04:48.830
just a pinch and put it
underneath their tongue,

00:04:52.917 --> 00:04:54.210
and let it absorb.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.713
NARRATOR: The coffee grinds aren't tasty.

00:04:56.713 --> 00:04:58.256
RUDY: That is not good.

00:04:58.256 --> 00:05:00.675
NARRATOR: But they do
dry up excess saliva,

00:05:00.675 --> 00:05:02.760
minimizing the sound of a wet mouth.

00:05:03.886 --> 00:05:06.514
A unique problem arises in the booth

00:05:06.514 --> 00:05:08.141
when actors are pronouncing words

00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:12.312
beginning with P, B, D, G, or T.

00:05:12.312 --> 00:05:13.938
JENNY SLATE: You're welcome Batman.

00:05:13.938 --> 00:05:15.606
SAMUEL L. JACKSON: Besides, I

00:05:15.606 --> 00:05:16.733
knew the cops would let you go.

00:05:16.733 --> 00:05:18.026
ALISON BRIE: Blast!

00:05:19.777 --> 00:05:21.487
NARRATOR: These sounds
are called plosives.

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:23.031
MATTHEW MCCONAUGHEY: And that's up!

00:05:23.031 --> 00:05:24.490
NARRATOR: It's these air vibrations

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.784
that are the foundation of beatboxing.

00:05:31.456 --> 00:05:33.833
But not so much in voice acting.

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:36.419
RUDY: That burst of air is
very loud and dramatic.

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:38.463
One of the simplest
ways to get rid of that,

00:05:38.463 --> 00:05:41.591
so if you have a pencil in
front of your microphone,

00:05:41.591 --> 00:05:44.510
and you're speaking, when
you say something like,

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.388
P it gets stopped by the pencil.

00:05:47.388 --> 00:05:49.057
NARRATOR: Contrary to
what people might think,

00:05:49.057 --> 00:05:51.559
voice acting can actually
be very physical.

00:05:51.559 --> 00:05:53.811
RUDY: You can conduct
yourself much the way

00:05:53.811 --> 00:05:56.856
a symphony conductor
works with an orchestra,

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:58.274
when he wants something legato

00:05:58.274 --> 00:06:01.110
and easy and when he wants big motions.

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:03.654
You can do that with
your hands and your body.

00:06:03.654 --> 00:06:06.949
If I wanted to say many moaning men,

00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:09.869
I want that to be legato
and smooth and loving,

00:06:09.869 --> 00:06:11.621
so I can use my hands like a conductor

00:06:11.621 --> 00:06:14.165
and go, many moaning men.

00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:18.252
If I wanted to be sharper,
I'd say many moaning men.

00:06:18.252 --> 00:06:20.171
JOAN: So your voice follows your body.

00:06:20.171 --> 00:06:22.840
RUDY: If I said take a Superman pose

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:26.427
and now say many moaning men
making money, it changes.

00:06:26.427 --> 00:06:27.929
If I say put your hands on a lectern

00:06:27.929 --> 00:06:30.348
and become the president then it's

00:06:30.348 --> 00:06:32.183
going to change the way you speak.

00:06:32.183 --> 00:06:33.976
If I say put your hands on your hip

00:06:33.976 --> 00:06:35.728
and start moving your head--

00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:36.562
JOAN: Then all of a sudden--

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:37.397
RUDY: Then now, yeah.

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:38.981
JOAN: You're gettin' down.

00:06:38.981 --> 00:06:41.442
And this often comes up in voice acting

00:06:41.442 --> 00:06:44.278
where the script is saying that you're at

00:06:44.278 --> 00:06:47.115
a baseball game, let's say,
and then there's atmosphere,

00:06:47.115 --> 00:06:49.367
you know, the fans are going crazy,

00:06:49.367 --> 00:06:51.702
but there's still
dialogue happening, right?

00:06:51.702 --> 00:06:53.746
One of the things that we have to do is

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:57.667
we have to talk over, as
if there's a crowd there,

00:06:57.667 --> 00:07:01.754
but that's not yelling, it is talking over

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:04.424
the crowd or the noise, right?

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:06.008
So I was working with someone

00:07:06.008 --> 00:07:09.303
and they just kept yelling
into the microphone

00:07:09.303 --> 00:07:12.098
so I finally said to 'em, use your hands

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:15.893
as if a wave was coming up and over

00:07:15.893 --> 00:07:17.979
so that while you're saying the line,

00:07:17.979 --> 00:07:22.150
you're gonna say the line as
if you're talking up and over.

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:26.237
So when he finally did
that, he was stunned.

00:07:26.237 --> 00:07:28.197
NARRATOR: In live action
films, the actors have

00:07:28.197 --> 00:07:31.200
sets, costumes, makeup,
and practical effects

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.619
to help them get into character.

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:37.623
In animated films, actors
have their imaginations.

00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:42.587
WILL ARNETT: I'm becoming,
I'm becoming I am Batman.

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:48.259
RUDY: When you're working
on a particular script,

00:07:48.259 --> 00:07:50.178
it may call for a certain mood.

00:07:50.178 --> 00:07:53.264
Maybe it starts with,
brr, it's cold in here.

00:07:53.264 --> 00:07:55.641
And so okay, this gonna
be a cold experience,

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:59.103
but you're in the booth, you
don't have people with you,

00:07:59.103 --> 00:08:00.646
you don't have props, you don't have

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:03.149
a real environment, and
you have to create a lot.

